1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,650
Chek Jawa: The Shore That
Was Saved by a Survey

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:07,779
In December 2000 a botanist
named Joseph Lai was guiding

3
00:00:07,859 --> 00:00:11,462
a group of schoolgirls
around Pulau Ubin when the

4
00:00:11,542 --> 00:00:15,525
tide went out at Chek Jawa,
the island's eastern tip.

5
00:00:15,525 --> 00:00:19,297
The flats it left behind
were alive with sea stars,

6
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:22,550
sea anemones, sponges, seagrass and crabs.

7
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:27,075
Lai later found out that the
shore was due to be reclaimed.

8
00:00:27,075 --> 00:00:31,534
Over the next year volunteers,
scientists and more than a thousand

9
00:00:31,614 --> 00:00:36,417
members of the public came to see
it, and on the 20th of December 2001,

10
00:00:36,497 --> 00:00:40,475
days before work was due to
begin, the Ministry of National

11
00:00:40,555 --> 00:00:44,750
Development announced that the
reclamation would be deferred.

12
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:49,447
Chek Jawa is still there today, a
boardwalk away from the Ubin jetty,

13
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:53,750
and it is still officially a
deferment rather than a reserve.

14
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:57,008
Pulau Ubin lies in the
Johor Strait off Changi,

15
00:00:57,088 --> 00:01:01,483
about 10 square kilometres of
hills, old quarries and villages,

16
00:01:01,563 --> 00:01:04,475
ten minutes by bumboat from Changi Point.

17
00:01:04,475 --> 00:01:08,673
Its name is usually traced
to a word for squared stone,

18
00:01:08,753 --> 00:01:13,575
and from the 1840s Chinese quarry
companies cut granite there.

19
00:01:13,575 --> 00:01:17,182
At its peak in the 1950s to the 1970s

20
00:01:17,262 --> 00:01:20,950
the island had about 2,000 residents.

21
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:25,175
By 2001 fewer than 200 were left.

22
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:29,500
Chek Jawa is the point at
the island's eastern end.

23
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:33,063
Although it covers only
about one square kilometre,

24
00:01:33,143 --> 00:01:38,206
it holds six distinct habitats: coastal
forest, mangrove, a sandy beach,

25
00:01:38,286 --> 00:01:41,634
sand flats with a seagrass
lagoon, coral rubble,

26
00:01:41,714 --> 00:01:46,000
and a tiny rocky island called
Pulau Sekudu, or Frog Island.

27
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,506
A marine biologist at the
National University of Singapore,

28
00:01:50,586 --> 00:01:53,459
Tan Koh Siang, has described it as one

29
00:01:53,539 --> 00:01:57,425
of the few estuarine
ecosystems left in Singapore.

30
00:01:57,425 --> 00:02:00,975
On the point stands an unusual building.

31
00:02:00,975 --> 00:02:05,257
House No. 1 was built in the
1930s as a holiday house for the

32
00:02:05,337 --> 00:02:09,618
colonial chief surveyor, Langdon
Williams, in the style of an

33
00:02:09,698 --> 00:02:14,552
English Tudor cottage, with black
timber framing, a steep tiled roof,

34
00:02:14,632 --> 00:02:18,350
a fireplace and corner
stones cut from Ubin granite.

35
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:23,275
After the British left it became
a local family's weekend home.

36
00:02:23,275 --> 00:02:27,456
Its last private owner was
the businessman Lee Thor Seng,

37
00:02:27,536 --> 00:02:30,825
and the government
acquired it in the 1990s.

38
00:02:30,825 --> 00:02:36,000
Singapore has been adding land
from the sea since the 1820s.

39
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,485
Its total land area grew from 581.5 square

40
00:02:40,565 --> 00:02:46,354
kilometres in 1960 to 682.3
square kilometres in 2001,

41
00:02:46,434 --> 00:02:50,593
and to 744.3 square kilometres by 2025,

42
00:02:50,673 --> 00:02:56,325
according to the Singapore
Land Authority's figures.

43
00:02:56,325 --> 00:03:00,089
The story of how that
began is told in an earlier

44
00:03:00,169 --> 00:03:03,700
post on the first
reclamations at Telok Ayer.

45
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:08,826
In 1992 the Urban Redevelopment
Authority approved a plan to

46
00:03:08,906 --> 00:03:14,025
reclaim the eastern tip of
Pulau Ubin, including Chek Jawa.

47
00:03:14,025 --> 00:03:18,617
The plan appeared again
in the 1997 development

48
00:03:18,697 --> 00:03:22,275
guide plan and the Master Plan 1998.

49
00:03:22,275 --> 00:03:25,073
The new land was to be "reserve land",

50
00:03:25,153 --> 00:03:28,405
eventually to be used
for military training,

51
00:03:28,485 --> 00:03:32,575
and the reclamation was
scheduled for the end of 2001.

52
00:03:32,575 --> 00:03:36,200
Before the reclamation began,
the National Parks Board

53
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:40,111
asked the Raffles Museum of
Biodiversity Research to make

54
00:03:40,191 --> 00:03:44,090
a salvage collection: a record
of what lived on the shore,

55
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:47,875
with photographs and specimens,
before it disappeared.

56
00:03:47,875 --> 00:03:51,628
The museum's teams surveyed Chek Jawa over

57
00:03:51,708 --> 00:03:55,175
six days in May, July and August 2001.

58
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:59,057
On the 11th of May 2001
the URA held a public

59
00:03:59,137 --> 00:04:03,107
forum on its Concept
Plan 2001, chaired by the

60
00:04:03,187 --> 00:04:07,325
Minister for National
Development, Mah Bow Tan.

61
00:04:07,325 --> 00:04:10,698
Joseph Lai spoke up about
Chek Jawa, and it was

62
00:04:10,778 --> 00:04:14,525
the first time the issue
had been raised in public.

63
00:04:14,525 --> 00:04:18,575
The press picked it up, and letters
followed from nature lovers,

64
00:04:18,655 --> 00:04:21,221
teachers and Ubin residents, many of them

65
00:04:21,301 --> 00:04:24,850
arguing that the shore would
make an outdoor classroom.

66
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:29,576
On the 27th of July 2001 the
URA replied in a letter to the

67
00:04:29,656 --> 00:04:34,625
Straits Times that the reclamation
would go ahead as planned.

68
00:04:34,625 --> 00:04:38,647
It explained that the land was
part of a long-term plan to

69
00:04:38,727 --> 00:04:42,900
make sure Singapore had enough
space for military training.

70
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:46,588
It cited a study commissioned
by the Housing and Development

71
00:04:46,668 --> 00:04:50,293
Board which had found that the
seagrass was patchy and that

72
00:04:50,373 --> 00:04:53,935
there did not seem to be a
resident population of dugongs,

73
00:04:54,015 --> 00:04:57,452
the sea mammals that feed on
it, and it said that NParks

74
00:04:57,532 --> 00:05:01,425
would transplant affected plants
to other parts of the island.

75
00:05:01,425 --> 00:05:04,075
The volunteers carried on.

76
00:05:04,075 --> 00:05:08,144
A group led by Joseph Lai
went on documenting the shore,

77
00:05:08,224 --> 00:05:10,885
and the Raffles Museum and the Nature

78
00:05:10,965 --> 00:05:14,150
Society (Singapore)
ran public walks there.

79
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:16,938
On the 20th of October 2001 more

80
00:05:17,018 --> 00:05:19,975
than a thousand people turned up.

81
00:05:19,975 --> 00:05:23,448
Eric Lin, a filmmaker,
recorded the survey for

82
00:05:23,528 --> 00:05:26,850
a documentary he called
Remember Chek Jawa.

83
00:05:26,850 --> 00:05:30,822
Over the second half of the
year the URA received field

84
00:05:30,902 --> 00:05:34,800
data and detailed reports
on the shore's biodiversity,

85
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:39,300
along with petitions, leaflets,
talks and photo exhibitions.

86
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:43,090
On the afternoon of the
20th of December 2001,

87
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:46,875
Mah Bow Tan met
representatives of nature and

88
00:05:46,955 --> 00:05:50,825
biodiversity groups and
members of the public.

89
00:05:50,825 --> 00:05:55,300
The same day his ministry
issued a short press release.

90
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:59,064
"The Ministry of National
Development (MND) has

91
00:05:59,144 --> 00:06:03,481
decided to defer the reclamation
works at Tanjung Chek

92
00:06:03,561 --> 00:06:07,650
Jawa originally slated for
end of 2001," it began.

93
00:06:07,650 --> 00:06:11,583
"This will allow MND to
discuss with relevant experts

94
00:06:11,663 --> 00:06:15,600
on how best the marine life
there can be protected."

95
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:19,117
The ministry said it had
considered all the public

96
00:06:19,197 --> 00:06:22,138
submissions and consulted the URA, NParks,

97
00:06:22,218 --> 00:06:26,742
the Housing and Development Board
and marine biologists from the

98
00:06:26,822 --> 00:06:30,850
National University of Singapore
and other institutions.

99
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:34,210
It also asked the public
not to rush to the shore:

100
00:06:34,290 --> 00:06:38,625
"there is no longer an urgent
need to rush to visit Chek Jawa."

101
00:06:38,625 --> 00:06:44,075
A second statement on the 14th
of January 2002 went further.

102
00:06:44,075 --> 00:06:47,822
Reclamation works on Pulau
Ubin would be deferred "for

103
00:06:47,902 --> 00:06:51,800
as long as the island is not
required for development".

104
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:55,684
Reclaiming only a small area
would not be cost-effective,

105
00:06:55,764 --> 00:07:00,901
the ministry said, and scientists from
the National Institute of Education,

106
00:07:00,981 --> 00:07:05,213
the Raffles Museum and the Nature
Society had recommended that

107
00:07:05,293 --> 00:07:09,675
any reclamation be kept very
small so as not to harm the shore.

108
00:07:09,675 --> 00:07:12,974
NParks would set up a
committee with the museum,

109
00:07:13,054 --> 00:07:17,700
the Nature Society and other experts
to plan how to look after it.

110
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:19,290
The news brought crowds,

111
00:07:19,370 --> 00:07:22,500
and the crowds became
a problem of their own.

112
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:27,346
NParks brought in a booking system,
marked routes and compulsory guides,

113
00:07:27,426 --> 00:07:31,600
and asked visitors to wear rubber
boots and to touch nothing.

114
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:34,728
About 20,000 people joined the

115
00:07:34,808 --> 00:07:38,550
guided walks between 2002 and 2007.

116
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:40,750
Then came the weather.

117
00:07:40,750 --> 00:07:44,845
Heavy rain in December 2006
poured fresh water over the

118
00:07:44,925 --> 00:07:49,778
flats and upset their salinity,
and much of the marine life died,

119
00:07:49,858 --> 00:07:53,350
including sea anemones,
sea stars and sponges.

120
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:56,520
In late March 2007 NParks suspended

121
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,850
the tours to let the shore recover.

122
00:07:59,850 --> 00:08:04,456
Chek Jawa reopened in July 2007
with new facilities costing

123
00:08:04,536 --> 00:08:09,300
6.2 million Singapore dollars:
a 1.1-kilometre boardwalk over

124
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:13,589
the mangroves and the flats,
a 20-metre viewing tower,

125
00:08:13,669 --> 00:08:18,909
and a visitor centre in House No.
1, which the URA had gazetted for

126
00:08:18,989 --> 00:08:23,675
conservation in 2003 and which
had been restored from 2005.

127
00:08:23,675 --> 00:08:26,662
The restoration won one of the URA's

128
00:08:26,742 --> 00:08:30,150
Architectural Heritage Awards that year.

129
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:34,525
Visitors no longer needed a
guide to walk the boardwalk.

130
00:08:34,525 --> 00:08:39,395
Three years later, on the 26th of
May 2010, two ships collided off

131
00:08:39,475 --> 00:08:44,570
Changi and spilled about 2,500 tonnes
of crude oil, which reached the

132
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:49,675
shore at Chek Jawa, and volunteers
were recruited to help clean it.

133
00:08:49,675 --> 00:08:53,280
Chek Jawa Wetlands is
managed today by NParks under

134
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:56,965
the Parks and Trees Act,
and the rules there forbid

135
00:08:57,045 --> 00:09:00,875
collecting any plant or
animal, fishing and swimming.

136
00:09:00,875 --> 00:09:05,025
It is not one of Singapore's
four gazetted nature reserves,

137
00:09:05,105 --> 00:09:07,892
though, and the land is still held under

138
00:09:07,972 --> 00:09:11,700
the deferral of 2002 rather
than set aside for good.

139
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:16,276
Government land-use plans in
2013 still marked Chek Jawa and

140
00:09:16,356 --> 00:09:20,931
parts of Pulau Ubin as reserve
sites, and later master plans

141
00:09:21,011 --> 00:09:25,900
have kept development of the
island as a long-term possibility.

142
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:29,477
The boardwalk, the tower and
the old house are open to

143
00:09:29,557 --> 00:09:32,592
anyone who takes a bumboat
across from Changi,

144
00:09:32,672 --> 00:09:36,600
and at a low spring tide the
flats are still there to see.

145
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:40,099
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has been

146
00:09:40,179 --> 00:09:43,476
reclaiming land for two
centuries, and much of its

147
00:09:43,556 --> 00:09:47,000
natural coastline has been
built over in that time.

148
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,843
Chek Jawa's reprieve came after
a botanist's chance visit,

149
00:09:50,923 --> 00:09:55,441
a museum's salvage survey and months
of documentation, which put the

150
00:09:55,521 --> 00:09:59,364
shore's value on record while
the decision was still open,

151
00:09:59,444 --> 00:10:03,975
and after the government weighed
that record and deferred the plan.

152
00:10:03,975 --> 00:10:07,649
More than twenty years on, it
is still a deferment rather

153
00:10:07,729 --> 00:10:11,205
than a reserve, and one of
the few places in Singapore

154
00:10:11,285 --> 00:10:14,775
where a natural shore of
this kind can still be seen.
